<%# The app-ladder row for /deployments: one CI testing card per reportable repo,
    showing where each application's suite sits on `accepted → release → main`.

    WHY IT EXISTS. An application's progress along the ladder is decoupled from the
    Next Release card. Work sits merged on `accepted` for hours before a sweep
    promotes it, and between releases Next Release reads "none active" while several
    repos quietly hold unshipped work. That in-between state had no surface at all.

    #app-ladder-row IS A STABLE SLOT, and that is load-bearing rather than cosmetic.
    The board takes live updates over Turbo Streams (`turbo_stream_from "deployments"`),
    and a stream can only replace a target it can name. Without this id the row was
    invisible to DeploymentsBroadcaster: the dev deploy tools (Open / Advance / Ship /
    Redraw / Reset) POST to /dev/board/<action>, which fires .release_modules, which
    replaces #current-release and #last-release — and nothing re-rendered this row, so
    it sat stale until a manual reload while every other card moved. The id plus
    DeploymentsBroadcaster.app_ladder is the fix; keep them in step.

    Locals: cards - Array<Ci::AppLadder::Card>, already sorted worst-first. %>
<% cards = local_assigns.fetch(:cards, []) %>
<%# data-rendered-at is the row's own render stamp, and it is diagnostic rather than
    decorative: it answers "when was this row last refreshed" for anyone looking at a
    board they suspect is stale — the precise question the missing broadcast made
    unanswerable. It is also what makes the wiring guard DETERMINISTIC. Asserting that
    the row's CONTENT changed is flaky by construction: opening a fixture release does
    not necessarily move any rung verdict or parked count, so an identical re-render is
    a legitimate outcome and the test would fail on a wiring that works. The stamp
    changes on every render, so it proves the MECHANISM — a broadcast arrived and
    replaced this slot — independent of whether the content happened to differ. %>
<div id="app-ladder-row" class="mb-6" data-test="app-ladder-row"
     data-rendered-at="<%= (Time.current.to_f * 1000).to_i %>">
  <% if cards.any? %>
    <div class="flex items-baseline gap-2 mb-2">
      <h2 class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-muted">Applications</h2>
      <span class="text-[10px] text-muted"><%= pluralize(cards.count, "repo") %> on the ladder</span>
    </div>
    <div class="grid min-w-0 grid-cols-1 gap-4 items-stretch sm:grid-cols-2 xl:grid-cols-4">
      <% cards.each do |card| %>
        <%# FULLY QUALIFIED on purpose. This partial is rendered two ways: inline from
            TasksController (where a bare "app_ladder_card" resolves against tasks/)
            AND standalone by DeploymentsBroadcaster via ApplicationController.render,
            where the same bare name resolves against application/ and raises Missing
            partial. Studio::Cable.safe_broadcast SWALLOWS that — so the broadcast
            failed silently and the row simply never moved, with nothing in the log to
            say why. tasks/_current_release carries the same fully-qualified habit. %>
        <%= render "tasks/app_ladder_card", card: card, card_class: "h-full min-w-0" %>
      <% end %>
    </div>
  <% end %>
</div>
